<!-- Author: Chrissy Safranski  -->

<p>
  Complete each of the following statements with an appropriate number or the symbols <m>\infty</m> or <m>-\infty</m>. Do your best to do so <em>without</em> using a graphing utility; instead use your understanding of the function's graph. To enter <m>\infty</m>, simply type the word <q>infinity</q>.
</p>

<ol marker="a.">
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \infty</m>, the function <m>e^{-t} \to</m></label>0</answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \infty</m>, the function <m>\ln(t) \to</m></label>infinity</answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \infty</m>, the function <m>e^{t} \to</m></label>infinity</answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to 0^+</m>, the function <m>e^{-t} \to</m></label>1</answer> <br/>
      Note that when we write <m>t \to 0^+</m>, this means that we are letting <m>t</m> get closer and closer to <m>0</m>, but only allowing <m>t</m> to take on positive values.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \infty</m>, the function <m>35 + 53e^{-0.025t} \to</m></label>35</answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \frac{\pi}{2}^-</m>, the function <m>\tan(t) \to</m></label>infinity</answer><br/>
      Note that when we write <m>t \to \frac{\pi}{2}^-</m>, this means that we are letting <m>t</m> get closer and closer to <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, but only allowing <m>t</m> to take on values that lie to the left of <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <answer><label>As <m>t \to \frac{\pi}{2}^+</m>, the function <m>\tan(t) \to</m></label>-infinity</answer><br/>
      Note that when we write <m>t \to \frac{\pi}{2}^+</m>, this means that we are letting <m>t</m> get closer and closer to <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, but only allowing <m>t</m> to take on values that lie to the right of <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>